  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nSignificant moisture advection with a lifting low out of the\nCarolinas (ahead of the western Great Lakes trough) has raised\ndewpoints near 70 while pumping PWAT values from 0.91 (~25th\npercentile) on the 00z sounding last night to 1.85 (~95th\npercentile) inches on the 12z sounding this morning. This moist\nairmass combined with diurnal heating will foster scattered\nshowers and thunderstorms this afternoon that wane after sunset\ntonight.\n\nFlash flooding is the main hazard to monitor as high PWAT,\nwarm-cloud rain process and weak forward motion can result in\nlocalized maxima that exceed 1-2\" in a short period of time.\nHowever, weak shear limiting storm duration and weak forcing \nliming storm coverage should play a large enough role to prevent\ntotals from reaching FFG (unless storms form over urban or\npoor-drainage areas) and keep any such occurrences very\nlocalized. Additionally, that lack of shear and forcing should\nprevent deep storm cores from tapping into drier air above 20kft\nto produce downburst wind potential; again, it could occur but\nlikelihood is low and would be very localized.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThe general storm environment outlook for today is likely to\nhold into early next week: moist airmass plus weak shear\nresulting in diurnally-driven thunderstorms that may have\nspottier coverage and limited damaging wind/flood risks. This is\ndue to the slow progression of an upper shortwave through the\nOhio River Valley that does little to change the entrenched\nwarm, moist airmass.\n\nDue to weak flow, the assessment of \"favored areas\" and \"hazard\nrisks\" is likely to remain more of a day-off duty as prior day's\nconvective evolution within and upstream of the region plays a\nlarger role in that day's outlook. Additionally, better modeling\nof small-scale embedded waves within the weak, broad eastern\nCONUS troughing in the day 1-2 range may capture subtle features\nthat could enhance thunderstorm coverage and hazard risks for a\ngiven day or even squelch such occurrences. The most that can be\nsaid at this time, then, is individuals should be aware that\neach day could see afternoon/evening thunderstorms worth\nmonitoring for potential damaging wind and/or flood risks, both\nwhich should be clearer the morning of.",
